package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val storyNicolBolas = StorySection(
    "nicol-bolas", "Nicol Bolas",
    "Nicol Bolas was the oldest and cleverest villain in the Multiverse, and the modern story is the record of his long climb back toward the godhood the Mending took from him.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "elder-dragons", "Elder Dragons",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "the-twins", "The Twins",
                    listOf(
                        "In the beginning of days on Dominaria the Ur-Dragon shed stones from its wings, and they fell to the ground like meteors, and from each stone one elder dragon hatched. From one stone came two. The twins woke together, named themselves together and touched the soil of Dominaria in the same instant, and the names they chose were Nicol and Ugin. They were half the size of their brothers and sisters. {The Ur-Dragon} is the parent none of them ever really knew.",
                        "The first thing they saw in the world was their sister Merrevia Sal being hunted down and killed by humans and their dogs. Nicol wanted to save her, and then to avenge her. [Ugin](who/ugin) held him back, for the sake of their own lives and for the sake of everything they had not yet learned. Nicol killed one of the hounds, and from that kill and from the fearless sun overhead he took a lesson he never let go of. The Multiverse was a place of hunters and the hunted.",
                        "When their brother Chromium remarked on the twins sharing their names between them, Nicol took a second name for himself, Bolas, so that he would be the equal of his larger siblings. When their sister Palladia-Mors mocked their size and their clumsy hunting, the twins spent years learning to hunt as a pair. Then they went to live with their brother Arcades Sabboth, who ruled a human settlement as its dragonlord. {Arcades Sabboth} and {Palladia-Mors, the Ruiner} are two of the siblings who looked down on them.",
                        "Ugin learned about the other worlds from an old holy woman there who spent her life thinking about things that could not be seen. [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) learned something more useful. He learned how humans thought, and how easily they could be turned.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dragon-killers-war", "Dragon-killers' War",
                    listOf(
                        "When he had learned enough, [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) took his twin back toward the mountain where they had hatched, to take revenge for their sister. The humans of that country had become dragon hunters, and they wounded and poisoned Ugin on the way. While Ugin recovered, Bolas went to work. He played the hunters' factions against each other until they were killing each other instead of dragons, and when all five of the great dragon-killers lay dead, the hunters made Bolas their supreme leader.",
                        "Then Bolas tried to use his brother the way he had used the humans, and [Ugin](who/ugin) understood, all at once, that Bolas had never cared for him at all. The shock of it ignited Ugin's spark. He vanished from Dominaria into the Meditation Plane, a quiet realm of water and thought, and from there into the wider Multiverse. Bolas believed the humans had killed him.",
                        "Ugin spent the next ages wandering, marvelling at worlds beyond counting. Bolas stayed home and built an empire. He learned to use human armies and human sorcerers, and he learned that dragons could be manipulated as easily as people if you knew what they wanted. Many human generations passed. The descendants of the elder dragon Vaevictis Asmadi raided Bolas's lands, and Vaevictis and his siblings came after him in person, and two of those siblings died for it.",
                        "After that Bolas changed his methods. He stopped fighting dragons and started whispering to them, and the dragon clans of Dominaria began to tear each other apart.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "elder-dragon-war", "Elder Dragon War",
                    listOf(
                        "The war that followed lasted for many human generations. [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) turned clan against clan. Palladia-Mors joined the fighting eagerly. Dragons crossed oceans to find new lands and new prey, raised war bands of the humans who worshipped or feared them, and gathered sorcerers who wanted a dragon's power for themselves. Even the wise Arcades Sabboth threw himself into the war, with Bolas's whispers in his mind. Bolas watched from a safe distance, confident that he would be the last one standing. {The Elder Dragon War} tells it as a saga.",
                        "By the end Bolas ruled half of Dominaria. The last battle was fought on the continent of Jamuraa, between his armies and those of Arcades, and it was going his way when [Ugin](who/ugin) came home. Ugin stepped between them to stop the slaughter, and Bolas, who had thought his twin dead, proudly showed him his empire and offered to share it.",
                        "Ugin told him that it was nothing, a speck in an infinity of worlds, and that he had found a way to walk between them. Bolas demanded to be shown how, and when Ugin could not teach him, accused him of hiding on Dominaria and plotting against him. The brothers parted in bitterness. In his rage and envy of what Ugin had become, Bolas's own spark ignited, and he planeswalked away from his half of the world and left the war unfinished. Of all the elder dragons only a handful survived it, Bolas, Arcades, Chromium, Palladia-Mors and Vaevictis among them.",
                        "Older stories had long told of an Elder Dragon War that few dragons survived, and the Chronicle of Bolas in 2018 gave the twins, their birth and Bolas's ignition the telling that is now official. The brothers had found the one thing they would fight over for the rest of time. They did not meet again for a very long while.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-meditation-realm", "The Meditation Realm",
                    listOf(
                        "When they did meet again it was on the Meditation Plane, the place where [Ugin](who/ugin) had first fled, and they fought there for days, then years, then generations, above its wide waters and on a long brawling path through other planes and back. In the end Ugin fell into the water, dying. Ugin later told it as a death he chose, letting his brother strike because he knew he could never match his hatred. The waves of his fall ran out past the edges of the realm and flung [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) through the Blind Eternities, through world after world, until he crashed back onto Dominaria in the island chain of Madara.",
                        "Ugin did not stay dead. His spirit woke in a new body made of the realm's own waters, and he became the Spirit Dragon, and he came out of that death with a purpose. He would guard the Multiverse from things like his brother, whose hatred would destroy whole worlds if no one stood against it. {Ugin, the Spirit Dragon} is the twin reborn.",
                        "Bolas, meanwhile, fought another planeswalker, a demonic leviathan, at Madara on the young plane of Dominaria. The battle lasted a month and broke the land to a third of its size. In the end the dragon won with his touch, and spent more than a year eating the leviathan's carcass and absorbing its power. All that was left was a pair of great bones that came to be called the Talon Gates, which Bolas thought of as his finest trophy.",
                        "The fight did something else, which no one understood for thousands of years. It made the first wound in Dominaria's time, the rift that would one day help bring the whole Multiverse to the brink in the [Time Rifts](time-rifts). And Bolas, without knowing it, had tied himself to that wound.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "god-emperor-of-madara", "God-Emperor of Madara",
                    listOf(
                        "Bound to the Madaran rift, [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) found he could not take body on Dominaria at all unless he anchored himself to its mana, or his presence would crack the plane. In Madara three lines of blue, black and red mana crossed at a single point. He killed the Empress of Madara and built his Imperial Shrine on that spot, and from it he ruled as God-Emperor for four hundred years.",
                        "His undoing was a man he made himself. Tetsuo Umezawa was his Imperial Champion, and after a series of trials Tetsuo renounced the title and swore to kill him, and killed his regent to prove it. In a rage Bolas left his body to follow Tetsuo's spirit into the Meditation Plane. It was a trap. While the dragon's mind was away, Tetsuo's Meteor Hammer spell destroyed the Imperial Shrine with Bolas's body inside it and cut him off from Dominaria completely. {Tetsuo Umezawa} is the champion who did it.",
                        "On the Meditation Plane, a realm of thought where size and strength meant nothing and where Tetsuo knew the ground far better than he did, Bolas lost. He was stripped of his power, his body and his mana, and killed. Centuries later he would swear to destroy the Umezawa line and every person who had ever helped it, and he meant it. For a long time, though, he could do nothing at all. A ghostly remnant of him lingered on the shores of Madara, caught between the world and the Meditation Realm by his tie to the rift.",
                        "That remnant waited for centuries, until the rifts began to grow and a lost young artificer with a strange spark washed up on Madara's beach. It was the beginning of his return, and the story of it is told in the [Time Rifts](time-rifts). The dragon who walked off that beach was a god again, for a few years, until the [Mending](the-mending) took it from him and he went looking for a way to win it back on [Alara](alara).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Chronicle of Bolas, by Kate Elliott, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Novel", "Emperor's Fist, by Scott McGough"),
                StorySource("Novel", "Time Spiral, by Scott McGough and Timothy Sanders"),
                StorySource("Set", "Legends, Dominaria United"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Nicol Bolas, Ugin, Elder Dragon War"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "alara", "Alara",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "five-shards", "Five Shards",
                    listOf(
                        "Alara was once a single rich plane, its mana tamed by great obelisks the ancient Alarans built. Then a planeswalker whose name no one knows came and plundered it, and the drain broke the plane like a prism breaks light. It split along the lines of its mana into five shards, each cut off from two colours of magic. Over the ages each became a world of its own. Bant of knights and angels. Esper of etherium and artifice. Grixis of the dead. Jund of dragons and endless hunting. Naya of jungles and behemoths.",
                        "[Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) came to Alara after the [Mending](the-mending) had broken him. He had lost his godhood and most of twenty-five thousand years of knowledge, and he set up in the dead shard of Grixis, in the necropolis of Kederekt. He knew something the shards did not. They were drifting back together, and the moment they met, the Conflux, would release a storm of mana large enough to make a dragon a god again.",
                        "He made sure it came sooner and harder. On Bant, the Order of the Skyward Eye spread fear along the borders. On Esper, the Seekers of Carmot warned that etherium was running out and set everyone hunting for a red stone that came from another shard. On Grixis the demon Malfegor gathered armies of the dead. On Jund the shaman Rakka Mar stirred the clans to greater and greater life hunts. On Naya the followers of the hero Marisi split the nacatl and broke their cities. And all the while his agents went after the obelisks that held each shard steady, and with each one the shards drew closer.",
                        "He gathered other servants too. On Esper an artificer named Tezzeret survived a death that should have killed him, ignited as a planeswalker and woke in Grixis, where Bolas offered him power for service. {Nicol Bolas, Planeswalker} is the dragon as he was in these years, smaller than he had been and far more patient.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-white-cat", "The White Cat",
                    listOf(
                        "On Naya there lived an albino nacatl named Ajani, an outcast among his people because his white fur was the colour of death. Only his brother Jazal cared for him. Jazal was kha of their pride in the Qasal Valley, and he took the title partly in the hope that his brother would be honoured for his sake. Ajani was the best healer in the pride, able to see into the souls of others, and was barely tolerated anyway. {Jazal Goldmane} is the brother who stood by him.",
                        "On the night of the Festival of Marisi a shadowy figure dropped a vessel of dark magic into the fire, and shades burst out of it and fell on the sleeping pride. Jazal was killed in his own lair. Ajani held his brother's body and heard Jazal's voice, and when he opened his one eye he was no longer on Naya. His spark had ignited.",
                        "He woke on Jund, and was saved from a dragon by a planeswalker named Sarkhan Vol, a dragon-shaman from Tarkir who had come to Jund looking for a dragon worth serving. He would soon find one in [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas), swear himself to the elder dragon, take his blood, and go off to wake the obelisk of Esper for him. He told Ajani to hold on to his anger and use it, and showed him how to walk back home. {Sarkhan Vol} is the dragon-shaman as Ajani met him.",
                        "Ajani took up his brother's axe, joined to his own, and went hunting for the killer. A fall nearly finished him and he woke on Bant in the care of a knight named Elspeth Tirel, and when he went back to Naya, the hero Marisi himself told him the name of the one he should be hunting. It was a dragon called Nicol Bolas. Ajani set out for Jund, the only place he knew to look for dragons, and found that Jund was no longer where it had been. The Conflux had begun.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("His pride denied him; his brother did not.", "Ajani, Nacatl Pariah"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-conflux", "The Conflux",
                    listOf(
                        "The shards crashed together like broken pottery forced back into one piece. Where they overlapped, jagged zones of mixed land appeared, and the peoples of Alara met for the first time in millennia as strangers. Esper's armies marched out to seize the red stone they had been told they needed. The undead of Grixis poured into the living shards to feed. Jund's warriors found whole new worlds of prey. Naya's armies followed the visions of their elf prophet Mayael, and Bant's knights met horrors swarming over their borders. {Conflux} shows the shards colliding.",
                        "At the point where all five shards met, a storm of raw mana of every colour spun up and would not stop. The Alarans called it the Maelstrom. Out of the reunited plane things woke that had not lived since before the Sundering, five-coloured beings like {Progenitus}, the soul of the world. And at the heart of it all [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) waited for the Maelstrom to grow large enough to eat.",
                        "Ajani found allies in the chaos. The Jund warlord Kresh and his clan swore to help him kill the dragon, and on the borders of Grixis Kresh settled his old grudge with Rakka Mar, though it was Ajani who finished her with a bolt of rage. Then Ajani learned the truth about his brother's death. The shaman Zaliki, his one friend in the pride, had summoned the shades that killed Jazal, manipulated into it by Marisi. Marisi was already dead at Zaliki's own hand. There was no one left to take revenge on.",
                        "Except the dragon behind Marisi, who chose that moment to show himself. The armies of Naya and Jund were facing each other on the field, Sarkhan Vol leading a flight of dragons on one side, when Bolas came to the Maelstrom to feed.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("After years of world-bending machinations, Bolas's triumph is at hand.", "Conflux"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-maelstrom", "The Maelstrom",
                    listOf(
                        "In front of both armies [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) drank the Maelstrom. All that wild mana poured into him, and for a moment the dragon who had been a god looked as if he might become one again. Ajani could not fight him. He did something no one expected. He cut every mana bond on the field, his own and everyone else's, and took for himself the last small scrap of the Maelstrom that Bolas had not swallowed.",
                        "With it he called up an effigy of Bolas's own soul. The two dragons, the real one and its perfect reflection, fought in perfect symmetry, and lunged and bit at the same instant, and in a flash of light both vanished. Bolas was banished from Alara. How badly he was hurt no one knows, but he had been beaten by a half-blind outcast carrying his dead brother's axe, and he never forgot it. For the rest of his schemes he built in some special plan to keep Ajani busy. {Ajani Vengeant} is the leonin in his fury.",
                        "Sarkhan Vol saw his master fall, and something in his mind broke. {Cruel Ultimatum} shows the dragon's mind closing on him. He would spend years afterward running errands for Bolas, half mad, and the worst of those errands would take him to Zendikar. Bolas had swallowed most of the Maelstrom, and it had not made him a god.",
                        "Ajani's pride offered him the title of kha and the honour he had always wanted, and he turned it down and gave it to Zaliki, and left Naya to walk the planes. In the years after the Conflux the shards learned slowly to live together. Bolas turned to a bigger prize, a prison on Zendikar whose secrets he had torn out of his brother's mind on [Tarkir](tarkir), long before.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("There is always a greater power.", "Cruel Ultimatum"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Shards of Alara, Conflux, Alara Reborn"),
                StorySource("Novel", "Alara Unbroken, by Doug Beyer"),
                StorySource("Story", "Shards of Alara web fiction and Planeswalker comics, magicthegathering.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Alara, Conflux, Ajani Goldmane, Sarkhan Vol, Nicol Bolas"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "tarkir", "Tarkir",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "ugins-grave", "Ugin's Grave",
                    listOf(
                        "Long before Alara, [Ugin](who/ugin) had made his home on Tarkir. His storms of magic gave birth to the plane's dragons, and he was fond of the humanoid clans too, so he gave them secrets to help them survive the dragons. When [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas)'s evil grew too great to ignore, Ugin joined the sphinx planeswalker Azor in a plan to trap him forever on the plane of Ixalan and then remove him from existence. Bolas found out. Azor had left traces of himself on the planes he ordered, and Bolas read them.",
                        "So Bolas came to Tarkir first. He found a willing helper in Yasova Dragonclaw, khan of the Temur, who wanted the dragons gone, and sent her a vision of a peaceful Tarkir littered with dragon bones. A great golden dragon with an orb floating between his horns promised her that if she led him to the Spirit Dragon's lair, he would kill Ugin, and the dragons would die with him. She marked the trail with a sign like a pair of curved horns.",
                        "The battle was close until Yasova turned Ugin's own dragons against him. Ugin fell into a canyon that had not been there before. Bolas tore through his brother's mind for everything he knew about the Eldrazi and the prison he had helped build for them on Zendikar, and then he left Ugin to die. Without Ugin, the dragonstorms stopped. The dragons of Tarkir dwindled and died out. Over the centuries the five clans rose to rule the plane in their place.",
                        "On Zendikar Bolas used what he had learned. He sent his broken servant Sarkhan Vol to guard the Eye of Ugin, and steered [Chandra](who/chandra) and [Jace](who/jace) there at the same moment, and the three of them together opened the last lock on the Eldrazi, as told in [Rise](rise). Sarkhan was left with Ugin's voice whispering in his head, and it led him home.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-dragon-man", "The Dragon Man",
                    listOf(
                        "Sarkhan Vol had been a general of the Mardu Horde under the warlord Zurgo. Sick of the endless blood, he had gone to the mountain shamans who worshipped the dragons Tarkir no longer had, and later, back at war, he was touched in a trance by an ancient dragon spirit. He sacrificed his own riders to unleash a spell that wiped the battlefield clean and ignited his spark. It was his first and last battle as a general. {Sarkhan, the Dragonspeaker} is the man who longed to be a dragon.",
                        "He came home, after [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) had broken him, to a Tarkir ruled by five clans. The Abzan held the deserts, the Jeskai the mountains, the Sultai the jungles, the Mardu the steppes and the Temur the frozen north, and the only dragons left were bones. Ugin's voice never left his head. Narset, khan of the Jeskai, found him wandering, led to him by visions of her own, and her calm brought his mind back for a while. He told her about other worlds and about the dragon who had killed Ugin a thousand years before. She told him that Ugin had been the soul of Tarkir, and that the plane had been out of balance since he died.",
                        "Narset agreed to take him to Ugin's tomb. They were attacked there by Zurgo Helmsmasher, who had never forgiven Sarkhan for the battle that made him a planeswalker. Narset held him off so that Sarkhan could reach the nexus of power around Ugin's bones, and when Sarkhan looked back, Zurgo landed a killing blow. With her last strength she told him to keep going. {Narset, Enlightened Master} is the khan who died for him.",
                        "Sarkhan went into {Ugin's Nexus} cursing Zurgo and grieving Narset, and in a blaze of light he was gone. He woke in the snow of a Temur tundra, alone, and Ugin's voice was silent at last. Then a storm came across the sky, and dragons flew out of it, and he understood that he was in the past.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("All at once Sarkhan's mind fell silent.", "Ugin's Nexus"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "fate-reforged", "Fate Reforged",
                    listOf(
                        "Sarkhan had come out twelve hundred and eighty years in the past, in the age when dragons still hatched from Ugin's storms. He watched the broodmother Atarka's young attack a Temur camp, and watched a Temur woman tear one of them out of the sky with a claw of red light. He followed her. She was Yasova Dragonclaw, and as she walked she burned a strange double curved sign into the ground behind her. {Yasova Dragonclaw} is the khan who was leading a dragon to Ugin.",
                        "She spared his life and let him follow, and he worked out slowly that she knew what would happen if Ugin died, that the storms would stop and the dragons would vanish. A voice in her visions had promised it. The voice belonged to a great dragon with scales like burnished gold and an orb between his horns. Sarkhan looked down at the marks she had been making and saw them for what they were. They were horns. [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) was coming to kill [Ugin](who/ugin), now, in this age, and Sarkhan was standing in the middle of it.",
                        "He took dragon form and flew for the eye of the storm. Bolas arrived as he did, and the two elder dragons fought, and Yasova cast her spell and turned every dragon's mind on its father. It gripped Sarkhan too, and he could only throw it off by giving up his dragon shape and falling from the sky. Broken, he crawled down into the new canyon and found Ugin dying. Ugin told him only that he would always fail so long as he sought guidance instead of truth.",
                        "Sarkhan had carried a shard of a hedron from the Eye of Ugin all this way. He woke it, and the stone unfolded into a great hedron cocoon that closed around the Spirit Dragon and held him on the edge of death. {Crucible of the Spirit Dragon} is the place where it happened. With that, history broke and remade itself, and in the history that was left, a man named Sarkhan Vol had never been born. He vanished.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-dragonlords", "The Dragonlords",
                    listOf(
                        "He came back to a present that had never happened before. With Ugin alive, the dragonstorms had never stopped, and over twelve centuries the dragons had beaten the clans. Five dragonlords ruled Tarkir now, Dromoka, Ojutai, Silumgar, Kolaghan and Atarka, and the very word khan was banned. No one remembered Sarkhan, not even Zurgo. He found that the journey had given him blue magic he had never had, and he went across the new Tarkir marvelling at its dragons. {Dragonlord Ojutai} is one of the five who ruled it.",
                        "Narset was alive. In this history she had been a gifted student of Ojutai himself, and had found buried records of the old clans and of the Spirit Dragon in secret chambers, and her spark had ignited as she learned the truth of the past. She did not know Sarkhan. When he told her what she had been to him in another life, she listened, and they set out together to find [Ugin](who/ugin).",
                        "Ugin had already woken. [Sorin](who/sorin) Markov had come to Tarkir, freed him from the hedron cocoon, and told him the Eldrazi were loose on Zendikar. Ugin asked at once for [Nahiri](who/nahiri), the third of the planeswalkers who had bound the titans, and grew angry when Sorin would not say what had become of her. He told Sorin to find her and meet him on Zendikar, which led to the [Battle for Zendikar](battle-for-zendikar). When Sarkhan reached him, Ugin had no memory of the timeline he came from, and decided that Sarkhan was a paradox, a dragon man who appeared from nowhere to save him and then appeared again twelve hundred years later. {Sarkhan Unbroken} is the shaman whole at last.",
                        "Sarkhan made his peace with that. Years later he helped Ugin take back the Meditation Realm from Bolas in the [War of the Spark](bolas-war-of-the-spark). And after the Desparkening took his spark and his dragon form, and the khans came back to Tarkir, he very nearly lost his mind again, in [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Khans of Tarkir, Fate Reforged, Dragons of Tarkir"),
                StorySource("Story", "Khans of Tarkir, Fate Reforged and Dragons of Tarkir stories, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Chronicle of Bolas, by Kate Elliott, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Sarkhan Vol, Ugin, Narset, Nicol Bolas"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "amonkhet", "Amonkhet",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "the-god-pharaoh", "The God-Pharaoh",
                    listOf(
                        "Shortly before the [Mending](the-mending), [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) came to the plane of Amonkhet, drawn by a strange blue metal called lazotep and the way it reacted with necromancy. Eight gods stood against him. Even with his power already waning he drove them back across their world until they made a last stand at the city of Naktamun. There one of them, Bontu, the god of ambition, betrayed the rest in secret, and Bolas broke through the city's barrier.",
                        "Then he cast a spell that killed every adult on the plane. With the gods weakened he twisted three of them into monstrous insect forms to serve him, and bent the minds of the other five to worship him, with Bontu keeping the spells in place in hope of a reward. He left the children to be raised by the mummified dead, and he left a religion behind him. The people of Naktamun were taught that their God-Pharaoh had gone away and would return when the second sun came to rest between the horns of his great monument. {Bontu the Glorified} is the god who sold her world.",
                        "Bolas also told them that he had made Amonkhet, and Liliana believed it, though Nissa later found that he had only corrupted a world that was already there. A great barrier called the Hekma kept the city safe from the wastes beyond, where the dead did not stay dead. Every life in Naktamun was arranged to end well. You trained, you were tested, you died gloriously, and the God-Pharaoh would come and give you the afterlife.",
                        "He left the plane like that for years, and walked away to other business. When he came back, everything would be ready.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-trials", "The Trials",
                    listOf(
                        "Children of Naktamun entered training as young as five, in groups called crops. Each crop spent years building an obelisk together, learning that one day they would grant each other glorious deaths. At the Ceremony of Measurement the five gods looked into their hearts and chose who would be initiates and who would serve the gods as viziers. The unworthy were driven out into the desert.",
                        "The initiates faced five trials, one for each god and each virtue. Solidarity under Oketra, knowledge under Kefnet, strength under Rhonas, ambition under Bontu, and in the last trial zeal under Hazoret, who killed the winners with her own spear. For each trial passed an initiate earned a cartouche of shining lazotep, and those who earned all five were the Worthy. Their bodies were carried through the Gate to the Afterlife to wait for the God-Pharaoh. They did not know that they were being made into Eternals, the perfect undead soldiers of a dragon's army, keeping every skill they had in life. {Oketra the True} and {Hazoret the Fervent} are two of the gods who judged them.",
                        "Some saw through it. A young initiate named Samut found older carvings in a forgotten district of the city, showing a history no one taught, and she announced to the whole crop that the gods and the trials and the God-Pharaoh were lies. She was sealed in a tomb of disgrace in the streets. {Samut, Voice of Dissent} is the initiate who would not stop saying it.",
                        "The Gatewatch came to Naktamun hunting Bolas, and freed her. [Liliana](who/liliana) came for a reason of her own. The demon Razaketh, the third of the four who held her contract, was waiting on Amonkhet, and she meant to kill him. The second sun was already sliding toward the horns.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("I'll fight no more just for the honor of dying. The afterlife will have to wait.", "Samut, on Samut, Voice of Dissent"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-hours", "The Hours",
                    listOf(
                        "When the second sun came to rest between the horns, the Gate to the Afterlife opened, and behind it stood Razaketh. At his command the river Luxa turned to blood. Through a hidden clause in their contract he took control of [Liliana](who/liliana)'s body and toyed with her, until the Gatewatch fell on him and she tore free. She raised the dead beasts in the bloody river and they tore the demon apart. {Razaketh, the Foulblooded} is the demon who guarded the gate.",
                        "Then the Hours came, one after another. The Eternals marched out of the necropolis, the Worthy dead in lazotep shells. The three twisted gods came back. The Scorpion God killed Rhonas in battle, and later Oketra and Kefnet as they tried to protect the people and repair the Hekma. The Hekma failed. The undead of the wastes poured into the city. {The Scorpion God} and {The Locust God} are two of the three Bolas had kept in reserve.",
                        "In the Hour of Devastation [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) arrived above his throne. He looked over his ruined city with pleasure and told Bontu to kill Hazoret, the last of the gods. Bontu beat her sister and brought her before him, and Bolas rewarded the god who had betrayed her world by setting the dead on her and having her torn apart. {Hour of Devastation} is his arrival.",
                        "Hazoret survived, gravely wounded, and in the wreck of the city Samut and a few others finished off the Scorpion God after Hazoret had broken its armour. When Hazoret thanked her, the joy of it ignited Samut's spark. But Bolas had not come to Amonkhet only for his army. He had come for the Gatewatch.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Everything here exists or perishes at my whim. Including you, Gatewatch.", "Nicol Bolas, on Hour of Devastation"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "defeat-at-naktamun", "Defeat at Naktamun",
                    listOf(
                        "He had been curious about them for a while, the group of planeswalkers who had banded together to fight the Eldrazi, and he waited to see what they would try. They tried everything. [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) knocked the invulnerable [Gideon](who/gideon) into the ruins again and again with his tail, ignored [Chandra](who/chandra)'s fire and Nissa's vines, and brushed aside [Liliana](who/liliana)'s zombies. He let [Jace](who/jace) into his mind for a moment and then crushed the telepath's thoughts. Something [Ugin](who/ugin) had hidden in Jace's mind long before tripped, wiped his memory and sent him away to Ixalan, which saved his life.",
                        "Bolas offered Liliana a way out. He would teach her to use the Chain Veil, if she left now and waited for him to call. She took it. Then he picked up Chandra and broke her ribs in his claw while lecturing her on the futility of using fire against a dragon, until she planeswalked away to save herself. He turned Nissa's bond with the land into a weapon against her and drove her off. Last he held Gideon in his claw, pierced his invulnerability, and gave him a choice, to stay and die or to live and break the oath he had sworn at Sea Gate. Gideon ran.",
                        "Bolas celebrated. The [Gatewatch](gatewatch) were scattered, his army was made, and the first steps toward his stolen power were taken. {Nicol Bolas, God-Pharaoh} is the dragon in that moment of triumph. He sent Tezzeret off to check on another servant on Ravnica and left the people of Amonkhet to the wastes.",
                        "Hazoret led the survivors into the desert. Later, when Tezzeret came back to Amonkhet to open a portal for the Eternals, Samut returned with a band of planeswalkers to shut it, and Hazoret gave them the spear she had been given by the God-Pharaoh himself, a weapon with a little of his own essence in it. That spear would matter, in the [War of the Spark](bolas-war-of-the-spark).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Amonkhet, Hour of Devastation"),
                StorySource("Story", "Amonkhet and Hour of Devastation stories, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Amonkhet (plane), Nicol Bolas, Hazoret, Razaketh, Samut, Oketra"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "bolas-war-of-the-spark", "War of the Spark",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "pieces-on-the-board", "Pieces on the Board",
                    listOf(
                        "The trap [Ugin](who/ugin) and Azor had once built for [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) became his weapon. The Immortal Sun lay hidden in a golden city on Ixalan, an artifact Azor had made from his own spark to keep planeswalkers from leaving. Bolas sent the gorgon Vraska to claim it, and Tezzeret carried it away through a portable planar bridge. Whoever held the Sun could close a plane like a fist. {The Immortal Sun} is the trap turned around.",
                        "Then he went to work on Ravnica. He turned guild leaders to his side one by one. Vraska ruled the Golgari with her memory of plotting against him taken away, Dovin Baan sat over the Azorius, and the planeswalker Kaya was hired to kill the Orzhov's ghost council and take their place. A Dimir agent slipped a thought into the mind of Niv-Mizzet, the dragon guildmaster of the Izzet, and Niv ordered Ral Zarek to turn an old device for detecting planeswalkers into a beacon that would call them to Ravnica to fight Bolas. Niv thought it was his own idea. {Interplanar Beacon} is the call that went out.",
                        "When Bolas came to Ravnica in person, Niv-Mizzet met him in the sky. The two dragons tore through the city with spell and claw and both drew blood, and Bolas's mind-shattering touch could not get past the Firemind's defences. In the end Niv collapsed a whole district on top of him, and Bolas came out of the rubble inside a dark sphere of death. Niv-Mizzet knew fear for the first time, and died. Only his skull and charred bones were left.",
                        "Bolas had not finished. His Planar Bridge stood ready, his army of Eternals waited on the other side of it, and the beacon was shining across the Multiverse. All he needed now was for the planeswalkers to come.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-elderspell", "The Elderspell",
                    listOf(
                        "They came by the dozen, answering the beacon, the [Gatewatch](gatewatch) among them. As soon as enough had arrived Bolas set the Immortal Sun burning and none of them could leave. The Planar Bridge opened, and through it from [Amonkhet](amonkhet) marched the Dreadhorde, the Eternals in their blue lazotep shells, led by [Liliana](who/liliana) as their general.",
                        "Then [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) cast the Elderspell, an ancient magic he had found in his study of Dominaria's deepest history. It dropped Ravnica into an unnatural night and gave every Eternal the power to see a spark. An Eternal only had to take hold of a planeswalker for a few seconds. The victim's body dried out, the Eternal glowed with stolen light until its lazotep cracked and it burst into flame, and the spark shot up into the sky like a purple comet toward the gem between Bolas's horns. Hundreds of planeswalkers died that way. {Spark Harvest} and {Dreadhorde Invasion} show the work being done.",
                        "With every spark Bolas grew. He towered over the city and became something close to a god again, a dragon who could bend reality around himself with a thought. Only the gods of Amonkhet and their weapons could still hurt him. {Nicol Bolas, Dragon-God} is the dragon at the height of his power, the closest he ever came to winning.",
                        "The story of the people of Ravnica, who fought and died in their streets beside the planeswalkers, is told in the Ravnica section, in its own [War of the Spark](ravnica-war-of-the-spark). Here it is enough to say that they fought, and that they bought time, and that the time was used.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-turn", "The Turn",
                    listOf(
                        "The defenders split into teams. [Karn](who/karn), Samut and two others went through the Planar Bridge to Amonkhet and shut it down from the far side, and came back with Hazoret's spear. Sarkhan Vol helped [Ugin](who/ugin) take back the Meditation Realm and carried the dead Niv-Mizzet's preserved mind into it for safekeeping, and the guilds of Ravnica, united for once, brought the Firemind back to life. {Niv-Mizzet Reborn} is the dragon who came back for a second fight.",
                        "[Gideon](who/gideon) killed the God-Eternal Rhonas with the Blackblade, then rode at [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) himself. The sword broke against the dragon's skull. Bolas had long ago enchanted it so that it would never harm an elder dragon again. Meanwhile [Jace](who/jace), [Teferi](who/teferi) and two others went to kill [Liliana](who/liliana), and Bolas saved her himself by tearing down the buildings they stood on.",
                        "Then, leading the Dreadhorde through the streets, Liliana watched a little girl shield her younger brother from a collapsing wall, and saw both of them crushed. She stopped. She turned her Eternals around and sent them against Bolas. He had warned her what would happen if she betrayed him, and he kept his word. He invoked the demons' contract, and she began to crumble into dust. {Liliana, Dreadhorde General} is the necromancer at the moment she chose.",
                        "Gideon reached her first. With his hieromancy he took the contract onto himself, and it killed him in her place. {Gideon Blackblade} is the man who died for her. Liliana, still standing, commanded the God-Eternals Oketra and Bontu to attack the dragon who had made them.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "bontus-bite", "Bontu's Bite",
                    listOf(
                        "[Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) laughed at them. He could repel a god with a thought. Then Niv-Mizzet, reborn, drove Hazoret's spear into his back. Bolas had given that spear to Hazoret himself, and it held a little of his own essence, which was why it could cut him when nothing else could. He found to his surprise that he could not simply wish it out of existence. He obliterated Oketra with a thought.",
                        "But the moment's distraction was enough. Bontu, the god who had betrayed her world to him, bit down on the dragon and drew out every spark he held, all the hundreds he had harvested and his own as well. It was too much for her to hold. Bontu shattered into pieces, and the sparks scattered into the aether and were gone. {God-Eternal Bontu} is the traitor who ended him.",
                        "The planeswalkers charged at the dragon, desparked and dwindling. Then [Ugin](who/ugin) spoke into [Jace](who/jace)'s mind. Bolas had cheated death once before, when Tetsuo Umezawa killed him, and Ugin himself had cheated death to become the Spirit Dragon. Killing Bolas was no guarantee of anything. Ugin asked Jace to cast an illusion of the dragon crumbling into ash, and Jace, unwillingly, did it. While everyone watched the illusion, Ugin planeswalked in and out again and took his brother with him.",
                        "Bolas had no spark now, and the Blind Eternities nearly killed him even wrapped in his brother's wings. It took him weeks to wake. When he did, he was in the Meditation Realm, and his brother was waiting.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-prison-realm", "The Prison Realm",
                    listOf(
                        "[Ugin](who/ugin) told his brother everything. The Meditation Realm had been his before it was ever Bolas's. He had helped bring Niv-Mizzet back. The gem that floated between Bolas's horns was a piece of Ugin's own soul, and through it he had watched every step his brother took and every mistake he made. And Bolas had lost the use of both his names, so that no one could ever summon him again. Ugin spread himself through the whole realm and became its jailer, and meant to stay there until his twin died of old age thousands of years from now. {Ugin, the Ineffable} is the Spirit Dragon as he was in those years.",
                        "For years only Ugin and Jace knew. The rest of the Multiverse believed [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) was dead. In the prison he faded, nameless, until he could barely remember who he was.",
                        "It did not hold. In the time of [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm), Narset and Elspeth stumbled into the Meditation Realm hunting the source of the storms and found [Jace](who/jace) already there. Elspeth spoke the dragon's name aloud, and it woke his memory. Jace stole the Spirit-Gem from Ugin, hoping to use the realm to undo the Phyrexian invasion and everything it had cost, Vraska's compleation first of all, and the realm was too much for him. It began to shatter. In the chaos Bolas rose laughing as his last bonds came undone, and both elder dragons vanished.",
                        "Ugin appeared on [Tarkir](tarkir) not long after, wounded and haggard, and told them the worst of it. His brother was named again, restored, and free somewhere in the Multiverse. After twenty-five thousand years Nicol Bolas was still not done.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Ixalan, Rivals of Ixalan, Guilds of Ravnica, Ravnica Allegiance, War of the Spark, Tarkir: Dragonstorm"),
                StorySource("Novel", "War of the Spark: Ravnica and War of the Spark: Forsaken, by Greg Weisman"),
                StorySource("Story", "Tarkir: Dragonstorm story, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Nicol Bolas, Elderspell, Immortal Sun, Interplanar Beacon, Meditation Realm"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
